1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,519
Synchronization taught us that locks give
both exclusion and visibility. That is a
strong tool. It is also heavier than some
problems need — and easy to misunderstand
as the only visibility story Java has.

2
00:00:12,639 --> 00:00:26,359
Here is a classic setup. One thread
prepares a packet of data — fills several
fields on a result object — then sets a
boolean ready flag to true. Another thread
loops until ready is true, then reads the
data.

3
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:36,719
Without a careful memory protocol, the
reader can see ready == true and still
observe stale fields. Or it can spin
forever on a cached false that never
updates in its view of memory.

4
00:00:36,839 --> 00:00:47,319
How do we publish "I am done writing" so
another thread reliably sees both the flag
and the data that came before it? volatile
is visibility tooling for that kind of
publication story.

5
00:00:47,439 --> 00:00:57,399
It is not a magic atomic wand for every
compound action. Look at this java for a
moment. The writer fills the data fields
first, then writes ready = true.

6
00:00:57,519 --> 00:01:09,599
The reader waits until it sees ready ==
true, then reads the data. The volatile
write and later volatile read create a
happens-before edge: actions before the
write become visible to a thread that
observed the read.

7
00:01:09,719 --> 00:01:21,879
Reverse the order — set ready first, then
write the fields — and you reopen the
window for a reader to see ready without
seeing the data. Happens-before is the
language Java uses for these guarantees.

8
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:31,960
You do not need to recite the entire Java
Memory Model in one sitting. You need the
habit of asking: which write is ordered
before which read, and by what rule?

9
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:42,980
Lock release happens-before a later
acquire of the same lock. A volatile write
happens-before a later read that sees that
write. Starting a thread happens-before
the first action in that thread.

10
00:01:43,099 --> 00:01:57,219
join completes after the dying thread's
actions. These edges are why concurrency
is more than "take turns on a CPU." What
volatile does not do is make compound
actions atomic: Look at this java for a
moment.

11
00:01:57,339 --> 00:02:05,339
count++ is still read-modify-write. Two
threads can both read 10, both write 11,
and lose an increment.

12
00:02:05,459 --> 00:02:12,340
Volatility may make each individual read
or write more visible; it does not merge
three steps into one atomic update.

13
00:02:12,459 --> 00:02:24,099
For counters, prefer atomics — which we
will treat properly soon — or locking when
the invariant spans more than one
variable. Another publication pattern: a
volatile reference to an immutable object.

14
00:02:24,219 --> 00:02:37,499
The writer builds a complete immutable
snapshot, then assigns it to a volatile
field in one write. Readers read the
reference once and use the snapshot
without further locking: Look at this java
for a moment.

15
00:02:37,619 --> 00:02:46,419
This pattern fails if the object is
mutable and still being changed after
publication — because then you are back to
sharing mutable state without a protocol.

16
00:02:46,539 --> 00:03:01,379
Using volatile on every field "for safety"
adds cost and still fails to protect
multi-step invariants like "transfer money
between two accounts." Overuse is not
caution. It is a sign the protocol was
never designed.

17
00:03:01,499 --> 00:03:06,739
Does volatile make ++ atomic? No. Use
atomics or locks for that.

18
00:03:06,859 --> 00:03:18,659
Use volatile when the need is visibility
and ordered publication — not when the
need is "update this counter safely under
contention." One more boundary: volatile
is about one variable's reads and writes.

19
00:03:18,779 --> 00:03:29,939
It does not freeze an entire object graph.
If you publish a volatile reference to a
mutable object and then keep mutating that
object from the writer thread, readers can
still see torn state inside.

20
00:03:30,059 --> 00:03:36,139
Immutability after publication — or
locking for ongoing mutation — remains
part of the design.

21
00:03:36,259 --> 00:03:45,899
Sometimes synchronized is almost enough,
but you need try-lock, timed waits,
multiple wait-sets, or fairness knobs the
intrinsic lock does not offer.

22
00:03:46,019 --> 00:03:54,619
That is when explicit locks enter — not as
a replacement for thinking, but as a
richer toolbox for ownership you must
manage yourself.
